{"qid":"Q1008","names":["Republic of Côte d'Ivoire"],"title":"Republic of Côte d'Ivoire","endonym":{"native":"République de Côte d'Ivoire","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Republic of Côte d'Ivoire gained independence from France on 7 August 1960 under Félix Houphouët-Boigny, who governed as president until his death in 1993 and built the country into the leading cocoa and coffee exporter in Africa through market-oriented agriculture and close ties with France. The 'Ivorian miracle' of sustained economic growth in the 1960s–70s gave way to debt crisis and structural adjustment in the 1980s, setting conditions for political instability after Houphouët-Boigny's death. The country experienced a coup in 1999, a civil war beginning in 2002 that partitioned it between government and rebel zones, and a second post-election crisis in 2010–2011 when Alassane Ouattara's internationally recognized victory over Laurent Gbagbo was enforced by UN and French forces. Ouattara subsequently served as president from 2011 onward.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial boundaries are precisely defined by the postcolonial border inherited from French West Africa and are fully attested.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Côte d'Ivoire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Cote-dIvoire"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ivory Coast","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivory_Coast"},{"label":"Tessy Bakary and others, UNESCO General History of Africa, Vol. VIII (UNESCO, 1993)","url":"https://unesdoc.unesco.org/ark:/48223/pf0000185055"}],"britannica":"place/Cote-dIvoire","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Ivory Coast","rulers":[{"